---
import ToolPage from '../../components/ToolPage.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Which solitaire is easiest to learn?',
    a: 'The comparator does not rank difficulty — that depends on the player and the ruleset. It lists rule differences; Klondike is the most familiar, FreeCell is fully face-up, and Spider uses multiple decks.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does NoCharge have Spider yet?',
    a: 'Not yet. Spider is a planned reference column, not a playable NoCharge game. It describes common 1/2/4-suit rules without promising a release date or final implementation.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the difference between draw-one and draw-three?',
    a: 'In Klondike, draw-one turns over one stock card per click; draw-three turns over three, and the waste pile cycles. NoCharge remembers the choice under nocharge:pref:klondike-draw-mode.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why does FreeCell call itself "all cards visible"?',
    a: 'Every card in FreeCell\'s tableau is face-up, so the game is about planning moves with free cells and empty columns rather than guessing about hidden cards.',
  },
];
---

<ToolPage
  title="Solitaire rules comparator"
  eyebrow="Klondike · FreeCell · Spider"
  description="Side-by-side rule table for Klondike, FreeCell, and planned Spider on NoCharge — decks, draw, tableau, foundations, and what each one makes you decide."
  path="/tools/solitaire-comparator/"
  category="Learning"
  bottomLine="The comparator lays out Klondike, FreeCell, and planned Spider in one table: decks, draw behavior, tableau, foundations, planning style, and local storage. It is rules comparison, not a difficulty ranking, and it links each entry to the live game or guide."
  faqs={faqs}
  embedCode={`<iframe src="https://nocharge.net/tools/solitaire-comparator/" width="400" height="640" title="NoCharge solitaire rules comparator"></iframe>`}
  nextActions={[
    { href: '/games/klondike/', label: 'Play Klondike', note: 'Draw-one or draw-three on a classic tableau.' },
    { href: '/games/freecell/', label: 'Play FreeCell', note: 'Every card visible; plan with free cells.' },
    { href: '/learn/glossary/#solitaire-foundation', label: 'Solitaire glossary', note: 'Foundation, tableau, stock, waste in one sentence each.' },
  ]}
>
  <section class="panel comparator-table" aria-labelledby="comparator-heading">
    <div>
      <p class="section-kicker">Rules side by side</p>
      <h2 id="comparator-heading">Classic solitaire families</h2>
    </div>
    <section class="comparator-scroll" aria-label="Solitaire comparison table" tabindex="0">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Rule</th><th>Klondike (live)</th><th>FreeCell (live)</th><th>Spider (planned)</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Decks</td><td>1 (52 cards)</td><td>1 (52 cards)</td><td>2 decks (104); 1, 2, or 4 suits</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Tableau</td><td>7 columns, increasing; top card face-up</td><td>8 columns, all face-up</td><td>10 columns; hidden cards with exposed top cards</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Draw</td><td>Stock to waste, draw-one or draw-three</td><td>No stock; cards are already distributed</td><td>One card dealt to each column per turn; no waste</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Foundations</td><td>4 foundations, Ace-up by suit</td><td>4 foundations, Ace-up by suit</td><td>8 completed King-to-Ace sequences, 13 cards each</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Key skill</td><td>Ordering with limited knowledge</td><td>Planning with complete knowledge</td><td>Building descending sequences within suits</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Local metric</td><td>Games won, best moves, draw preference</td><td>Games won</td><td>No NoCharge metric; not playable</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Guide</td><td><a href="/guides/klondike/">Klondike guide</a></td><td><a href="/guides/freecell/">FreeCell guide</a></td><td>Not yet available</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>
    <p class="comparator-note">Spider rows are a reference to common rules, not a specification or release promise. Read the live Klondike and FreeCell pages for their exact implemented rules.</p>
    <h3>Comparison reading</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Klondike</strong> combines visible moves with hidden tableau cards; draw mode changes which waste cards are available.</li>
      <li><strong>FreeCell</strong> exposes the whole deal from the start. Planning uses complete card information, but deals still vary and solvability is not guaranteed.</li>
      <li><strong>Spider</strong> (planned) uses two decks and removes complete descending same-suit sequences. Suit count changes the sorting task, not the total of 104 cards.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <Fragment slot="how">
    <p>This is a static rule reference, not a deal simulator. Read across a row to compare the same feature: card count, hidden information, stock behavior, or foundation goal. Klondike and FreeCell link to playable NoCharge implementations; Spider is a reference for a planned, not currently playable, game.</p>
    <h3>Worked example</h3>
    <p>In both Klondike and FreeCell, a black 7 can sit on a red 8 in the tableau, while a foundation needs the same suit in ascending order. An empty tableau column differs: Klondike needs a king, but FreeCell accepts any card.</p>
    <p>FreeCell's sequence capacity is (empty free cells + 1) × 2 to the power of the spare empty columns. With one free cell and one spare empty column, the capacity is (1 + 1) × 2 = 4 cards. An empty destination column is not also counted as spare workspace. Klondike does not use this free-cell capacity rule.</p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="limits">
    <ul>
      <li>The table does not inspect a live deal, certify solvability, suggest a move, or rank the games by difficulty.</li>
      <li>Classic solitaire has variants. The linked NoCharge guides and game-page rules describe the shipped implementations; the Spider column is not a promise about a future release.</li>
      <li>Seeing all FreeCell cards removes hidden information, not variation between deals. It does not mean every random deal is solvable.</li>
      <li>This comparison has no saved choices or metrics and remains readable without JavaScript.</li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ToolPage>

<style>
  .comparator-table {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 1rem;
    padding: 1.25rem;
    min-width: 0;
  }

  .comparator-table > div:first-child {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 0.35rem;
  }

  .comparator-scroll {
    max-width: 100%;
    min-width: 0;
    overflow-x: auto;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: 0.6rem;
  }

  .comparator-scroll:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--accent-strong);
    outline-offset: 2px;
  }

  .comparator-scroll table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 0.84rem;
    text-align: left;
  }

  .comparator-scroll th,
  .comparator-scroll td {
    padding: 0.5rem 0.6rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    vertical-align: top;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  .comparator-scroll th {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .comparator-scroll a {
    color: var(--accent-strong);
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  .comparator-note {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
  }

  .comparator-table h3 {
    margin-top: 0.4rem;
    font-size: 1rem;
  }

  .comparator-table ul {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 0.45rem;
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: 1.2rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.86rem;
  }
</style>
